{
 "what_this_is": "The tracked population of coverage assertions -- asserts in test/check functions whose population is READ FROM THE REPOSITORY. Regenerated by tools/coverage_assertion_sweep.py and checked against it by tests/phase1/test_coverage_assertion_sweep.py, which fails if this list and the current code disagree.",
 "why_it_is_tracked": "The enumerator's definition was wrong three times in one round, and was corrected each time only because somebody remembered which assertions had to be in the set. That knowledge does not survive to the next person, so the population is pinned rather than recalled.",
 "the_criterion": "An assert inside a function named test_* or check_* is IN when the function reads the repository -- directly (ls-files, rglob, glob, read_text, read_bytes, os.walk, subprocess.run), through a module helper that does, or through a FIXTURE that does. The criterion MUST catch `assert not <collection>`, the shape the first definition missed, and MUST catch a test receiving its population from a fixture, the shape the third definition missed. Both are known-positives in the test file.",
 "the_probe": "tools-side: the empty-population probe replaces each population helper with an empty result and runs the test. A test that still PASSES is a CANDIDATE, never a finding -- an empty population is sometimes a legitimate vacuous truth and only a per-assertion judgment separates them (R222's shape).",
 "scope_NOT_covered": "The probe covers ONE vacuity subclass. It does not cover VALUE-COINCIDENCE (a wrong value matching data already present, as \\b27\\b matched inside TB-27) -- emptying the population makes those fail correctly, so the probe passes them. It does not cover WRONG-INPUT (an assertion never exercising its subject, as the bare=True control passed a frame where a dict was expected).",
 "measured_at_commit": "c9f4e53",
